the bitcoin realized cap has hit an  all-time high now when most people think  of bitcoin they think of the price of  one bitcoin today it trades around 55  000  and somebody may say to you the bitcoin  asset is a trillion dollars and when  they're talking about a trillion dollar  asset what they're talking about is the  market cap itself now the way that the  market cap is calculated is you simply  take all of the bitcoin that is in the  circulating supply and you multiply it  by the price of one bitcoin so let's say  uh that there was 18.9 million bitcoin  in circulation times 55 000 price point  that gets you the market cap so  outstanding number of bitcoin in  circulation times the actual price of  one bitcoin gets you that market cap but  sometimes that's not necessarily the  best metric to actually understand what  the value of bitcoin is so there's  something called the realize cap  the bitcoin realized cap is looking at  the last time that that bitcoin actually  traded hands so let's say for example  that somebody bought one bitcoin at  thirty thousand dollars rather than that  one bitcoin counting as fifty five  thousand dollars towards the market cap  that one bitcoin simply would count as  thirty thousand dollars towards the  realized cap the last time that a it was  traded hands was at a certain price  point if somebody buys a bitcoin today  it will count as the realized cap is  fifty five thousand dollars so wherever  the last time that that bitcoin actually  changed hands that is the amount that  counts towards that realized cap and so  what this does is basically shows the  realized amount of the bitcoin well that  has hit an all-time high as well so not  only do we have the market cap crossing  over one trillion dollars but we now  have the realized market capitalization  breaking 400 billion dollars so even  though tons of people have bought  bitcoin over the years and held it we  know that 85 of all bitcoin in  circulation right now hasn't changed  hands in the last 90 days we still hit  an all-time high in the realized market  capitalization and so the realized  market capitalization will always be  lower than the actual market cap but  it's a really important metric to  understand this specific chart that  we're going to show you now is the  realized market analysis this comes from  a bitcoin magazine article dylan leclair  a couple other folks put this together  and i love this chart because what it  shows is green yellow and red green  shows that there's an uptrend and we're  in a bull market the yellow is showing  some sort of plateau a consolidation uh  really the accumulation phase right  before a massive bull run and then red  is the downtrend or the bear markets and  seeing go all the way back there to 2011  or so you can see that we started in the  green we go massive bull market we go  bare then we go sideways accumulation  mini bull run more accumulation second  leg to the bull run then in 2015 or so  bear market really long bear market  almost two years year and a half two  years we then go into the accumulation  phase right before 2016 bam 2017 hits  bull run go back into the bear market in  uh 2018 2018 to 2019 pretty much all  bear market bottom out around 3 000  but then in the beginning of 2019 we  basically start to go sideways again the  cycle restarts and so in 2019 to 2020  all accumulation we then get that bull  run at the end of 2020 that most of you  are here to experience go all the way up  to 64 000. we then go back into a little  bit of a  bear market there go all the way down to  30k about 50 draw down or so but then we  go back into accumulation so once we go  back into accumulation we continue to  consolidate sideways and with history  repeats every single time you have that  accumulation the next thing that follows  is another move upwards and so we've  never had an accumulation phase followed  by a bear market it's always  accumulation  bull market and then we go into a bear  and so it's important to kind of  understand how history has played out  again it's not a predictor of the future  but if you look at this you can start to  understand some of the market cycles you  can start to understand how some of the  price movements actually occur and what  you can really understand from this  realized price analysis is that we are  in that consolidation phase right now so  pretty bullish if you were to look over  the next couple of months  then lastly we have uh this realized  price market analysis it's just simply a  little bit zoomed in on that specific uh  time period so if you go back to kind of  beginning of 2020 you can see right  there that accumulation phase and then  with the great coloring you can just see  bam into that bull market uh starting in  kind of october of 2020 goes from about  10 11 000 all the way up to 64 000  uh ultimately peaks out at 64. and we  kind of go into that uh that bear market  now that bear market phase what's really  interesting about that is if you were to  look at the market cap  you would actually see a very  significant drawdown if you were to look  at the price per one bitcoin the us  dollar exchange value we went from 64  000 down to 30 thousand dollars 28  thousand dollars but when you look at  the realized price what you can tell is  that there actually wasn't that level of  volatility the reason why this is much  smoother is because most people were not  actually selling on the way down so that  realized price doesn't move for those  that aren't selling the only time that  the realized price moves is when bitcoin  is actually trading hands and so now you  can tell that even though there was a  slight downtrend not quite as severe as  the price or the market cap there was  still a slight uh downturn we then go  right back and the realized price starts  to rise again and that's ultimately  leading us today to reach that all-time  high and that realized price on a per  bitcoin basis just over 21 000 so pretty  cool to see uh the actual data when you  start to unpack it not only saying that  the uh us dollar exchange price kind of  bitcoin equals uh one bitcoin equals 55  000 is bullish not only looking at  things like the market cap crossing over  a trillion dollars that's bullish not  only looking at all of the social  signals or the regulatory regime in  terms of focusing they're not going to  ban it that's bullish also looking at  the on-chain metrics looking at who's  buying and who's selling that's bullish  long-term holders saying 85 of all  bitcoin hasn't moved in the last 90 days  that's bullish and then when you look at  the realized market price as well you  can see that that is bullish  additionally and so when we start to  look at this uh part of what's so  beautiful about bitcoin is that you  really couldn't do this type of analysis  with a traditional security it would be  almost impossible for you to know every  single asset that traded hands at what  price and be able to to uh kind of keep  track and understand what a realized  price was with this level of accuracy
 

